#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/id #uuid "3913d667-ede5-5fee-ba67-ff64afeb7883" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087433 :question/id #uuid "4f0593d8-bce5-5c73-aedd-ffe0faf15a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-14364" :question/problem "The lines $y=cx+7$ and $y=5x-3$ are parallel. What is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "353a2fb8-5677-5047-8f39-595f43d9eeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087687 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087434 :question/id #uuid "b2f31785-e173-518c-a1fd-b069e0834b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14367" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3?$\n\nI. $y=4x-8$\n\nII. $y=12x+1$\n\nIII. $y=3x-12$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-y & =3 \\\\ -y & =-12x+3 \\\\ y & =12x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is $y = 12x+1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bf8c8b-64f1-5552-a59e-78eef021c122" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087688 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087435 :question/id #uuid "88c5f90c-9bf9-58a2-a9a2-7c7ff1fc4f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14370" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =-1 \\\\ -2y & =-kx-1 \\\\ 2y & =kx+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\dfrac{k}{2}=2.$ Consequently, $k=4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43401830-730c-57d7-8831-9a0580042ffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters"}] :topic/math-academy-id 837 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050744}] :topic/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane"}]}
